    <!-- Section 3: The Science of Lossy vs. Lossless Compression -->
    <section id="lossy-vs-lossless" aria-labelledby="science-heading" class="mb-14">
      <h2 id="science-heading" class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-extrabold text-[#4C4541] dark:text-[#FCF0DA] mb-4">
        3. The Science of Lossy vs. Lossless Compression
      </h2>
      <p class="text-base text-[#4C4541]/85 dark:text-[#FCF0DA]/85 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Understanding the mathematical mechanisms behind image compression allows web developers and designers to achieve maximum page speed without sacrificing graphic aesthetic quality.
      </p>

      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 mb-8">
        <!-- Lossless Card -->
        <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl bg-[#FCF0DA] dark:bg-[#282220] border border-[#4C4541]/15 dark:border-[#FCF0DA]/15">
          <div class="inline-block px-3 py-1 rounded-lg bg-[#AEAC78]/25 text-[#4C4541] dark:text-[#AEAC78] font-bold text-xs uppercase mb-3">
            Lossless Compression
          </div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-[#4C4541] dark:text-[#FCF0DA] mb-2">Exact Pixel Preservation</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-[#4C4541]/80 dark:text-[#FCF0DA]/80 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Lossless algorithms (used in PNG and Lossless WebP) work identically to ZIP file compression. They identify repetitive pixel sequences, strip non-essential metadata (camera EXIF, GPS coordinates, date stamps), and encode color tables using Huffman and DEFLATE encoding.
          </p>
          <div class="text-xs text-[#4C4541]/75 dark:text-[#FCF0DA]/75 space-y-1.5 border-t border-[#4C4541]/10 dark:border-[#FCF0DA]/10 pt-3">
            <p><strong>Artifacts:</strong> Zero loss of data. Every pixel is bit-for-bit identical.</p>
            <p><strong>Compression Ratio:</strong> 10% to 30% reduction.</p>
            <p><strong>Best For:</strong> Logos, icons, UI wireframes, line drawings, and screenshots with crisp text.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Lossy Card -->
        <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl bg-[#FCF0DA] dark:bg-[#282220] border border-[#4C4541]/15 dark:border-[#FCF0DA]/15">
          <div class="inline-block px-3 py-1 rounded-lg bg-[#F2C46A]/30 text-[#4C4541] dark:text-[#F2C46A] font-bold text-xs uppercase mb-3">
            Lossy Compression
          </div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-[#4C4541] dark:text-[#FCF0DA] mb-2">Perceptual Optimization</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-[#4C4541]/80 dark:text-[#FCF0DA]/80 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Lossy algorithms (used in JPEG and WebP) exploit the biological limits of the human eye. The eye is far more sensitive to luminance (brightness) than chrominance (color variations). By discarding high-frequency color harmonics that humans cannot distinguish, massive file size savings are unlocked.
          </p>
          <div class="text-xs text-[#4C4541]/75 dark:text-[#FCF0DA]/75 space-y-1.5 border-t border-[#4C4541]/10 dark:border-[#FCF0DA]/10 pt-3">
            <p><strong>Artifacts:</strong> Minor mathematical variance; completely imperceptible at 80%+ quality.</p>
            <p><strong>Compression Ratio:</strong> 70% to 90% reduction.</p>
            <p><strong>Best For:</strong> Complex photographs, ecommerce product galleries, hero banners, and portfolio shoots.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

